From: pyadi-dt release validation
Subject: [PATCH] jesd204: skip callbacks for unregistered devices

Device unregister leaves a topology node in the graph but clears fsm_inited.
A later client's stop traverses the entire graph for state and completion callbacks, even
nodes whose driver was already unloaded. Their dev_data can point into freed
module memory. This was reproduced while removing AD9371 and ADRV9009 after
unbinding their AXI IIO consumers.

Core exit also visits nodes that clients already unregistered; skip their
already-destroyed sysfs/device state.

Only invoke driver callbacks on registered clients, as the normal probe
path already requires. Do not change the overlay notifier's safety checks.

Base: ADI Linux 2e8908932dfd9faf40ec9220a508aa1ce02699a8 (6.1.70).

diff --git a/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-fsm.c b/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-fsm.c
--- a/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-fsm.c
+++ b/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-fsm.c
@@ -1151,6 +1151,11 @@
 	enum jesd204_state_op_reason reason;
 	struct jesd204_link_opaque *ol;
 
+	/* Keep rollback traversable after another client unregisters. */
+	if (!jdev->fsm_inited)
+		return fsm_data->rollback ? JESD204_STATE_CHANGE_DONE :
+			JESD204_STATE_CHANGE_DEFER;
+
 	jesd204_fsm_handle_stop_state(jdev, link_idx, fsm_data);
 
 	state_op = &jdev->dev_data->state_ops[it->table[0].op];
@@ -1332,6 +1337,10 @@
 {
 	const struct jesd204_link * const *links = fsm_data->cb_data;
 
+	/* Unregistered clients may belong to an already unloaded module. */
+	if (!jdev->fsm_inited)
+		return JESD204_STATE_CHANGE_DONE;
+
 	if (!jdev->dev_data->fsm_finished_cb)
 		return JESD204_STATE_CHANGE_DONE;
 
diff --git a/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-core.c b/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-core.c
--- a/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-core.c
+++ b/drivers/jesd204/jesd204-core.c
@@ -1189,7 +1189,7 @@
  */
 static void jesd204_dev_unregister(struct jesd204_dev *jdev)
 {
-	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(jdev))
+	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(jdev) || !jdev->dev.parent)
 		return;
 
 	jesd204_dev_destroy_sysfs(jdev);
